[{:seon.task/name "a-merged-tree-is-analyzed-in-a-fork"
  :seon.task/plan "From an independent audit of Context's merges (2026-11-08), approved by the owner; owner, 2026-11-08: forking is cheap, so check the program main would be after the merge in a fresh env. Two defects, read in the code. (1) seon.writer/unanalyzable runs source/refresh-tx in root's own image, so before a merge is decided root evaluates the candidate's ns form (which can require), its defmacros, unaliases live namespaces and flips a live var's :private (seon.source/alias-held-not-declared guards one symptom of this). (2) :merge-candidate analyzes one branch over main, and seon.writer/octopus-merge then merges every member whose keys are disjoint, with no analysis of the combined tree: branch A retracts [:var f], branch B adds [:var g] calling f; both are judged against the same main, both merge in one commit, and main does not load. Context's replay (lab root, 2026-11-08) merged them one at a time, so the second went :behind and was refused at analysis; the octopus order is untested and is the red proof here. The design: a fork of root (copy-on-write, the precompiled program) is given the merged value's text of the changed namespaces (doc/files), removes those namespaces and loads their text, plus every namespace that uses a changed macro (a macro is expanded at its caller's compile; a var call goes through the var), from the call graph; it answers per form {:ok} or an error as data and ends. Root loads no candidate code. The octopus merge computes its combined candidate first and that is what the fork analyzes. Reproduced 2026-11-08: seon.reconcile-test/an-octopus-merge-analyzes-its-combined-tree is red on main, both members merged in one commit. Analyzing the combined tree in the analyzing process does not fix it, tried: refresh-tx resolves x/f against the live namespace, which still holds f after the change removed it (resolve answered true), since re-reading a file only adds definitions. So no analysis in a live image sees a removed name; the same holds for one branch removing a function an unchanged file on main still calls (not yet a test). The existing mechanism for a process that matches a record is seon.program/load-changes!: it removes each definition the ops leave with no form, loads each changed form with what a changed macro expanded into, and answers error maps. The child: the candidate committed to a merges branch, a fork from the fork server (root forks nothing: its threads refuse a fork), which applies load-changes! of main to the candidate and answers its errors; open whether a removed var's unchanged callers are reloaded (load-changes! loads only changed forms, so a caller compiled earlier keeps the unmapped var until called). Measure before deciding: ticks per changed file in the fork against refresh-tx in root on the 17-merge replay; coverage of :refer, :import and :as against today's analysis; then compare with the var-digest check at done and with checking each eval as it is recorded. Cost line: n = changed files plus macro users; O(n) loads after an O(1) fork (about 0.3 M ticks). If it holds, delete seon.source/alias-held-not-declared, the in-root analysis in seon.writer/unanalyzable, and seon.reconcile/judgment's :gate path for merges that reach no process test. A merge-blocking check: its numbers go to the owner before it lands. See docs/merging.md#a-merge-runs-what-the-change-reaches."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}]
